WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.080
The first film showed an outside death receptor building a platform to gather

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:13.900
caspase-8. What if danger starts inside the cell, like damaged DNA, with no outside

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:20.200
signal? Inside the cell, the platform's two parts are kept in separate rooms so

4
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.560
nothing fires by mistake.

5
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:27.923
One part, a protein called APAF-1, floats folded and quiet in the cytosol.

6
00:00:29.203 --> 00:00:34.383
Its partner is cytochrome c, the small protein that normally carries electrons

7
00:00:34.383 --> 00:00:35.943
inside mitochondria.

8
00:00:37.243 --> 00:00:42.483
Keeping cytochrome c locked behind the outer mitochondrial membrane stops any

9
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:43.663
platform from forming.

10
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:47.323
Here, escaping that room is the death signal.

11
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:53.447
A protein family named after BCL-2 controls that outer membrane.

12
00:00:54.727 --> 00:00:55.967
It has three classes.

13
00:00:57.507 --> 00:01:00.807
Effectors called BAX and BAK can open pores in the membrane.

14
00:01:02.387 --> 00:01:06.627
Guards like BCL-2 hold them in check, gripping them in a surface groove.

15
00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:13.287
Sensors share only one short helix with the family, called BH3, which fits that

16
00:01:13.287 --> 00:01:14.307
groove. . . . .

17
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.770
Notice the double negative: sensors promote death by blocking the guards that

18
00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.130
restrain BAX and BAK.

19
00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.610
Some sensors can also activate BAX and BAK directly.

20
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:31.930
Now suppose mild stress makes a few sensors, fewer than the spare guards waiting on

21
00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:36.850
the membrane. Pause and predict: does the outer membrane open yet?

22
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:37.850
Pause and predict.

23
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:49.533
Not yet. Spare guards soak up that small wave of sensors, so BAX and BAK stay

24
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:53.653
clamped. That buffer protects the cell from minor noise.

25
00:01:54.893 --> 00:01:59.293
When sustained stress makes enough sensors to fill the guards, BAX and BAK break

26
00:01:59.293 --> 00:02:04.833
free. They assemble into pores and open the outer mitochondrial membrane.

27
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:10.003
Through those openings, cytochrome c pours out into the cytosol.

28
00:02:11.243 --> 00:02:17.323
With the nucleotide dATP, it makes seven copies of APAF-1 open up and join into a

29
00:02:17.323 --> 00:02:21.363
wheel. That seven-spoke wheel is the apoptosome.

30
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:28.163
Just like the surface DISC, its hub gathers caspase-9, whose paired copies then cut

31
00:02:28.163 --> 00:02:29.723
and unlock executioners.

32
00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.607
The outside and inside routes also connect.

33
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:39.347
Active caspase-8 from the surface platform can cut a sensor named BID.

34
00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:44.527
Cut BID travels to the mitochondrion and helps trigger BAX and BAK.

35
00:02:45.727 --> 00:02:48.387
That pulls the mitochondrial gate into the response.

36
00:02:49.887 --> 00:02:53.787
Inside, guards, sensors, and effectors together set the threshold.

37
00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:00.450
So the outer membrane usually gives way only after sensors fill the free guards.

38
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:06.590
Once sensors tip the scale, cytochrome c escapes, the wheel assembles, and

39
00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.110
irreversible cutting begins.

40
00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:15.330
In the end, the BCL-2 family balance decides whether mitochondria release cytochrome

41
00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:15.670
c.
